WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:09.915
Were you the young child that 
never wanted to get your hair done?

00:00:09.915 --> 00:00:13.394
You never wanted to put a comb
or a brush through it?

00:00:13.394 --> 00:00:16.865
You were more than happy 
with your hair being wild,

00:00:16.865 --> 00:00:17.765
free,

00:00:17.765 --> 00:00:19.727
in its natural state?

00:00:19.727 --> 00:00:21.155
Well this was me.

00:00:21.155 --> 00:00:24.646
I hated, I mean hated 
getting my hair done.

00:00:24.646 --> 00:00:28.597
And every other Saturday,
I would have to go get my hair done.

00:00:28.597 --> 00:00:33.226
Some days I would get twists, 
some days I would get braids,

00:00:33.226 --> 00:00:37.007
but for the most part, 
I got my hair straightened.

00:00:37.007 --> 00:00:42.177
The results were always beautiful, 
but the process was quite traumatic.

00:00:42.177 --> 00:00:44.816
Because my hair was so thick,

00:00:44.816 --> 00:00:49.726
I would go through hours of boredom 
and even sometimes pain.

00:00:49.726 --> 00:00:54.552
At first, it was my mother dragging me 
to these gruesome appointments,

00:00:54.552 --> 00:00:58.887
but over time, I started to crave
these hairstyles for myself

00:00:58.887 --> 00:01:01.936
and it became a part of my routine.

00:01:01.936 --> 00:01:06.378
Why did I go through this over 
and over again?

00:01:06.378 --> 00:01:09.048
It’s just hair, right?

00:01:09.048 --> 00:01:12.879
Well, how many of you guys
have gone to a hairstylist

00:01:12.879 --> 00:01:17.532
and they cut off way too much
of your hair?

00:01:17.532 --> 00:01:24.078
Or how many of you guys have been 
embarrassed just by a bad hair day?

00:01:24.078 --> 00:01:26.698
The truth is it isn’t just hair.

00:01:26.698 --> 00:01:30.248
We all put extra time 
and effort into our hair

00:01:30.248 --> 00:01:32.789
because it means something to us.

00:01:32.789 --> 00:01:38.430
And as a young African American woman,
I've been taught the art of manipulation.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:43.379
Growing up, I have not only put 
a generous amount of money,

00:01:43.379 --> 00:01:46.540
but time and energy into
changing my hair.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:52.569
I've had an abundance of the hairstyles,
but my go to was the relaxer.

00:01:52.569 --> 00:01:54.543
For those of you who do not know,

00:01:54.543 --> 00:02:00.275
the relaxer is a chemical treatment that
permanently straightens curly coily hair.

00:02:00.275 --> 00:02:03.881
Here are two pictures of me as a child.

00:02:03.881 --> 00:02:06.714
The one to the left, 
I was about five weeks old

00:02:06.714 --> 00:02:10.314
and the one to the right,
I was about six or seven years old.

00:02:10.314 --> 00:02:11.723
The picture of me as a baby

00:02:11.723 --> 00:02:15.113
is one of the only pictures I have 
of my natural hair.

00:02:15.113 --> 00:02:19.824
The rest of my childhood pictures 
are of me with relaxed hair.

00:02:19.824 --> 00:02:25.454
With relaxed hair, my hair was more 
manageable, more presentable.

00:02:25.454 --> 00:02:27.994
Natural hair doesn’t lay flat.

00:02:27.994 --> 00:02:32.565
It doesn’t even grow downward, 
but instead outward like a tree.

00:02:32.565 --> 00:02:36.183
And because of this, 
my hair was inappropriate,

00:02:36.183 --> 00:02:40.685
and I never questioned this fact, it was
just something I knew to agree with.

00:02:40.685 --> 00:02:45.905
Now, I always knew how to maintain
my hair in these hairstyles,

00:02:45.905 --> 00:02:50.254
but I was never taught how to love 
the hair that I was born with.

00:02:50.254 --> 00:02:52.972
And I believe that is the main problem.

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:57.663
You have to love parts of yourself 
in order to love your whole self.

00:02:57.663 --> 00:03:03.033
Now, why aren’t young black girls 
taught to love their hair?

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:06.873
Well, there was a time where all 
black women were natural.

00:03:06.873 --> 00:03:11.554
They didn't have access to chemical
treatments or tools to straighten it.

00:03:11.554 --> 00:03:15.584
There were kinks, curls, and coils
all throughout their hair,

00:03:15.584 --> 00:03:19.736
until someone told them 
that it wasn’t beautiful.

00:03:19.736 --> 00:03:23.095
Not just someone, but multiple people.

00:03:23.095 --> 00:03:25.318
As much as we hate to bring it up,

00:03:25.318 --> 00:03:29.207
slavery created 
a Eurocentric standard of beauty.

00:03:29.207 --> 00:03:33.207
This means that only Eurocentric 
features were deemed beautiful,

00:03:33.207 --> 00:03:40.826
such as light skin, pointy noses, 
and last but not least, straight hair.

00:03:40.826 --> 00:03:45.986
Black women gave into this false pretense 
and decided to create some solutions.

00:03:45.986 --> 00:03:48.286
Although previous methods were used,

00:03:48.286 --> 00:03:52.544
the hot comb became one of 
the most popular methods to be used,

00:03:52.544 --> 00:03:56.986
which is kind of comparable 
to a modern flat iron.

00:03:56.986 --> 00:04:02.042
But then the holy grail was discovered,
the relaxer.

00:04:02.042 --> 00:04:06.025
This product swept African American 
consumers off of their feet

00:04:06.025 --> 00:04:08.296
and it started flying off the shelves.

00:04:08.296 --> 00:04:14.124
Not only did women use this product,
but men did, too.

00:04:14.124 --> 00:04:18.845
I mean, y'all, James Brown had a relaxer.

00:04:18.845 --> 00:04:22.186
This compliance to altering our own hair

00:04:22.186 --> 00:04:25.717
contributed to the underlying 
hatred of natural hair

00:04:25.717 --> 00:04:29.127
And now, African Americans 
were brainwashed

00:04:29.127 --> 00:04:31.842
to believe that their 
own hair was too nappy.

00:04:31.842 --> 00:04:33.587
It's too wild.

00:04:33.587 --> 00:04:39.788
It isn’t thought to be long enough 
or even in some cases professional.

00:04:39.788 --> 00:04:44.405
It isn’t good hair because 
of its texture and thickness.

00:04:44.405 --> 00:04:48.125
Now, even today, I hear fellow 
black women saying,

00:04:48.125 --> 00:04:50.756
“Well, natural hair isn’t for everybody.”

00:04:50.756 --> 00:04:53.493
Well what is that supposed to mean?

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:56.578
The hair that grows out 
of my scalp isn't for me?

00:04:56.578 --> 00:05:00.497
The hair that I was born with
isn’t for me?

00:05:00.497 --> 00:05:05.408
As silly as I might sound,
the relaxer created this thought,

00:05:05.408 --> 00:05:09.138
and people thought that it made 
good hair attainable.

00:05:09.138 --> 00:05:12.619
It made the impossible possible.

00:05:12.619 --> 00:05:16.923
Even I got entangled 
into the magic of this product.

00:05:16.923 --> 00:05:21.951
With long, straight hair, 
I was beautiful and accepted.

00:05:21.951 --> 00:05:25.790
I was so focused on 
how I was being perceived.

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:31.800
Black girls were known 
for having short nappy hair, but not me.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.204
I started to place the burden on myself

00:05:34.204 --> 00:05:37.499
of being the black girl 
with the long good hair.

00:05:37.499 --> 00:05:40.904
I loved being the person
that defied that stereotype.

00:05:40.904 --> 00:05:46.556
And because of this, I wore my hair 
straight for the majority of my life.

00:05:46.556 --> 00:05:52.186
It wasn’t until high school that I thought
maybe this way of thinking is flawed.

00:05:52.186 --> 00:05:55.287
I saw a revival in the natural
hair community.

00:05:55.287 --> 00:05:58.754
Black women were starting 
to ditch the relaxers.

00:05:58.754 --> 00:06:02.809
All down my YouTube timeline 
was natural hair videos.

00:06:02.809 --> 00:06:08.958
Businesses began to release more 
products than ever catered to ethnic hair.

00:06:08.958 --> 00:06:13.587
So, I thought, 
"Why not switch it up? I'll go natural."

00:06:13.587 --> 00:06:17.313
I went natural strictly 
to experiment with my hair,

00:06:17.313 --> 00:06:23.217
but I never knew the self-loving journey
that it would take me on.

00:06:23.217 --> 00:06:26.338
In order to go natural, 
you can do so in two ways:

00:06:26.338 --> 00:06:29.589
you can either big chop or transition.

00:06:29.589 --> 00:06:34.968
A big chop is when you cut off 
all the relaxed hair and start fresh.

00:06:34.968 --> 00:06:38.999
However, transition is when 
you let the hair grow out over time,

00:06:38.999 --> 00:06:40.659
gradually cutting it.

00:06:40.659 --> 00:06:43.599
And I somewhat chose both choices.

00:06:43.599 --> 00:06:45.867
For about a year, I transitioned.

00:06:45.867 --> 00:06:51.480
But as I was about a year in, 
the detangling became too hard for me.

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:55.531
My roots were so curly
and my ends were just stick straight.

00:06:55.531 --> 00:06:59.350
So I thought, I was like, 
"I'm going to cut off all of my hair."

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:03.220
And my cousin and I snuck out 
to a hair cuttery,

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:05.921
defying my mother’s wishes,

00:07:05.921 --> 00:07:12.761
and as I was in the chair, I felt good.
I felt empowered. I felt in control.

00:07:12.761 --> 00:07:15.631
Society wasn’t going to control me.

00:07:15.631 --> 00:07:18.391
I was going to lead by example.

00:07:18.391 --> 00:07:22.032
However, as soon as I stepped 
out of the chair,

00:07:22.032 --> 00:07:25.857
that amazing feeling soon faded away.

00:07:25.857 --> 00:07:26.992
I thought to myself,

00:07:26.992 --> 00:07:29.131
"How could I go from feeling so powerful,

00:07:29.131 --> 00:07:32.941
and then the next minute, 
feel so powerless?"

00:07:32.941 --> 00:07:37.102
I looked in the mirror 
and didn’t recognize myself.

00:07:37.102 --> 00:07:39.692
I had to face the real me.

00:07:39.692 --> 00:07:45.122
I never knew how much I was hiding 
behind my hair until that day.

00:07:45.122 --> 00:07:52.334
I was never an insecure person, 
but I started to lack confidence.

00:07:52.334 --> 00:07:56.043
My proud title was taken away from me.

00:07:56.043 --> 00:08:01.424
Mother knows best, right? Because I felt 
that I had just made a huge mistake.

00:08:01.424 --> 00:08:05.723
All of my life, I had long, straight hair.

00:08:05.723 --> 00:08:09.034
Now I had short, curly hair.

00:08:09.034 --> 00:08:14.654
This was more than a haircut for me; 
this was a lifestyle change.

00:08:14.654 --> 00:08:18.814
I was no longer subscribing 
to societal demands,

00:08:18.814 --> 00:08:23.046
but I wasn’t prepared 
for the consequences of that, either.

00:08:23.046 --> 00:08:26.784
Some people liked my hair, 
some people didn’t.

00:08:26.784 --> 00:08:29.594
Some of my friends said 
how brave it was to do that,

00:08:29.594 --> 00:08:32.045
and how they just loved the look of it.

00:08:32.045 --> 00:08:34.505
They said it was so cute
and it was so curly.

00:08:34.505 --> 00:08:39.456
On the other hand, I had comments that
I looked way better with straight hair.

00:08:39.456 --> 00:08:44.137
They would say, "Why’d you cut your 
hair off? It was so beautiful."

00:08:44.137 --> 00:08:46.487
Keyword: "was."

00:08:46.487 --> 00:08:49.786
My hair was no longer deemed beautiful,

00:08:49.786 --> 00:08:52.226
and the negative comments stung

00:08:52.226 --> 00:08:56.406
because I was still trying 
to love my natural hair myself.

00:08:56.406 --> 00:09:01.416
All of the YouTubers I had watched fell
in love with their natural hair instantly,

00:09:01.416 --> 00:09:03.948
so why didn’t I?

00:09:03.948 --> 00:09:05.616
But I thought to myself,

00:09:05.616 --> 00:09:12.868
"Why don't people like natural hair?
But more importantly, why don't I?"

00:09:12.868 --> 00:09:17.008
And the answer is it's because
were taught not to.

00:09:17.008 --> 00:09:21.528
I had to train myself 
to think like and for myself,

00:09:21.528 --> 00:09:23.787
It didn’t matter what people thought.

00:09:23.787 --> 00:09:25.877
It was my hair.

00:09:25.877 --> 00:09:31.448
If they didn't like the real me, 
then they didn’t like me at all.

00:09:31.448 --> 00:09:34.659
I started to love and accept my own hair 
for what it was.

00:09:34.659 --> 00:09:37.228
I love the untamed look of it.

00:09:37.228 --> 00:09:41.879
The way my hair defies gravity 
is something that I think is beautiful.

00:09:41.879 --> 00:09:45.168
Now, I think, 
"Why would I go back straight?

00:09:45.168 --> 00:09:47.598
Why would I ever do that to myself?

00:09:47.598 --> 00:09:50.419
Why would I fight who I am anymore?

00:09:50.419 --> 00:09:52.509
I'm not going to do it.

00:09:52.509 --> 00:09:58.087
And today, we live in a world full of 
constant manipulation in all areas,

00:09:58.087 --> 00:09:59.638
not just hair.

00:09:59.638 --> 00:10:02.529
We have such access to technology

00:10:02.529 --> 00:10:06.671
that makes it so easy 
for you to change yourself.

00:10:06.671 --> 00:10:09.277
If you don’t like your body,
get plastic surgery.

00:10:09.277 --> 00:10:12.224
If you don’t like your crooked teeth, 
you can get braces.

00:10:12.224 --> 00:10:14.582
But hair is different.

00:10:14.582 --> 00:10:16.553
Yes, you can manipulate it,

00:10:16.553 --> 00:10:22.532
but you can cut if off 
and start all over at any point in time.

00:10:22.532 --> 00:10:26.752
And at one point, society is going 
to try to straighten your hair;

00:10:26.753 --> 00:10:29.755
It’s going to try to curl your hair,

00:10:29.755 --> 00:10:32.613
try to manipulate your hair.

00:10:32.613 --> 00:10:35.284
And it's going to tell you 
not to love your hair,

00:10:35.284 --> 00:10:38.504
and ultimately, not to love yourself.

00:10:38.504 --> 00:10:42.665
Now, this is how I wear 
my hair 90% of the time.

00:10:42.665 --> 00:10:46.755
By no means, is it perfect, 
but it’s a part of me.

00:10:46.755 --> 00:10:48.884
Some days, it might be elongated.

00:10:48.884 --> 00:10:52.276
Some days it might be curled up 
in a little bush.

00:10:52.276 --> 00:10:56.601
And that is something 
that I am totally okay with.

00:10:56.601 --> 00:11:02.380
Time to time, I will still straighten 
my hair, change up my look

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:07.200
because I do love the versatility 
that African Americans have created.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:10.391
There is nothing wrong 
with changing your look,

00:11:10.391 --> 00:11:13.352
but the key lies in managing that look.

00:11:13.352 --> 00:11:17.330
Wear a different look, 
but don’t let that look become you

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:20.482
because you are good enough, naturally.

00:11:20.482 --> 00:11:21.822
Thank you.

